A||N||16||Vlad||Strange idle.....  (Question).||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||11-15-2000||08:13 PM||||Hello<P>I noticed some kind of problem with idle that never mentioned in discussion. <P>Generally, car drives very good. However, if I open throttle up to 3-3.5 K while in PARK and then turn engine off, next time I start the idle is very high, about 2000 rpm. Once I put it in drive and start moving the tachometer shows everything normal; however, it feels like the engine pushes car forward. (Hard to stop and keep it steady in traffic lights). This continues  no meter now hot engine is. If I put it in Neutral , it idles about 2000 rpm, but during the next minute it goes down to regular 800-900.<P>The strange thing is that if I don't floor it before turning off, the above situation doesn't ever happen. <P>What is this? Is this normal?<P>Can it be connected somehow to my poor gas mileage (15 MPG)?<P>Thanks and waiting for input.<P>85 Fiero GT V6 Auto<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Vlad (edited 11-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Vlad (edited 11-15-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||varnster||11-15-2000||08:59 PM||||keep it simple, take a look at the carb see if something might be sticking,thats what it<BR>sounds like, Im just a shady tree mech, but<BR>9 times out if 10 its something simple, Go<BR>through your cables and linkages,and rule<BR>all that out.Hope you find it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||11-15-2000||09:45 PM||||I had a similar problem.  The car would start up, and revv right up to sometimes almost 3000 and never want to come down.  I sprayed carb cleaner all over the TBI, and that seemed to help, but then it started doing it again.  I read a "saturday mechanic" article about EGR systems, and thought "If the EGR isnt working properly, maybe the AIR/FUEL is very rich, causing the engine to revv"  (I have a 4cyl by the way) Well, I checked the EGR, and it was broken, so i replaced it.  Now when the engine is cold, it will revv to 2000, hardly ever any more, and then after about 30 seconds at the most go down to 800-900.  If the engine is still warm and then started, it never even thinks about going up to 2k.<BR>Also, TPS (throttle positioning sensor), maybe it's getting stuck.  Just some thoughts.<P>------------------<BR>"The fish who keeps on swimming is the first to chill upstream"||||reg||1
Z||000003||KnightRyder31||11-15-2000||10:35 PM||||Fieros dont have carbs.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||11-15-2000||10:41 PM||||I had the high rev problem , car seemed posessed at times. After fixing a bad headlight isolation relay , which also caused flickering lights , crackles on radio , and idle fluctuation as well as high rev idle,it hasn't happened since. Coincidence? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ROB MAC||11-15-2000||10:43 PM||||Oh , mine has a carb.( '84 )||||reg||1
Z||000006||Vlad||11-15-2000||10:46 PM||||EGR sysem has been fixed and now worked properly. Thanks to boneyards businesses......<P>This smells like something electrical or sensorical ( how about this word...))..).<BR>I don't have funds now to buy a new sensor as a precaution. I've got to figure out what causes trouble and then hit it with money.<BR> If TPS sensor is going bad, would it affect anything else in addintion to what have been described?<BR> How can I check TPS for proper operation?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Terrybogin||11-15-2000||10:47 PM||||I know everyone has heard about the guy who told the doctor, "Doctor, it hurts when I do this."<BR>The doctor then says, "Well, don't do that."<P>Well- if it only idles crazy after you floor it and shut the engine off, dont do that!<BR>The engines idle up slightly after reving them, if you are shutting it down before it comes back down to a normal, steady idle the computer is probably not able to find it's idle reference when you restart. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Vlad||11-15-2000||10:52 PM||||POB MAC<P>The bad electrical connections may be the reason for almost anything. God knows what parts of the car may be affected by Babum living in wires.<BR>In my case, very poor backround light on the dash was caused by absense of negative ground cable. Who knows what else was affected...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Vlad||11-15-2000||10:59 PM||||Hey guys<P>Look what a great answer and explanation I got.<BR>&gt;&gt;<BR>Yes that is normal and what you are actually doing is opening the idle air<BR>control valve (IAC) without giving it a chance to "reset" or adjust which it<BR>does everytime you return to idle. Since you shut the car off - the ECM has<BR>no idea where it was supposed to be so leaves it where its at (high idle)<BR>until the car is driven above 40mph at which time the ECM resets the IAC<BR>(like it always does) and the car should idle fine. The two factors which<BR>produce this effect are the engine speed and the simultaneous key off -- not<BR>something I would recommend doing just to "try it out".<BR>&gt;&gt;&gt;<P>Seems to be true.||||reg||1
Z||000010||KnightRyder31||11-16-2000||09:03 AM||||NO FIERO's HAVE CARBS!!!<BR>Rob, I have an 84 also, we both have the same car, we both have TBI(throtle body injection) which is a form of fuel injection.<BR>This is very mistakable with a carb. But unless your fiero has a diff. engine, you have a TBI.||||reg||1
Z||000011||ROB MAC||11-16-2000||06:10 PM||||Instead of a float it has an injector . Other than that it looks like a carb to me.<BR>A MUTANT carb perhaps?||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||11-16-2000||08:10 PM||||The simplest answer is likely the right one....... You're likely causing this simply by punching it then shutting it off. Doing that can confuse the ECM. The ECM expects to be at idle when you shut it off, not 2000+ RPM.<P>Once RPM is over about 2000 RPM the ECM closes off the IAC. Just what result that has when you shut off the car at high rpm depends on the specific ECM and what chip programming is in it. <P>The older ECMs have slower processors and ar often crabby about aberant conditions. So when you do somehting like this the ECM has to relearn the IAC. This likely won't set a code but will screw up idle.<P>Patient: Doctor, It hurts when I do this.<BR>Doctor: Well then don't do that.||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTBro||11-17-2000||03:29 PM||||My 86 GT is also experiencing idling problems.  Most of the time when I start the car, whether it is cold or hot, the engine idles around 2000 RPM.  I know that is high, but I was content with it.  I am even more content with the 1500 RPM it used to run at.<P>But lately, especially after driving on the highway for about 25 miles, the engines idles at 2500-3000 RPM.  Needless to say, it is starting to aggravate me.  I have not changed the O2 sensor since I bought the car in 1999.  I know the EGR tube is riddled with holes.  These could be most of the problem, right?<P>BTW, I recently replaced the fuel pump and the fuel pressure regulator.||||reg||4
Z||000014||theogre||11-17-2000||06:53 PM||||gtbro yes, your EGR hardware is likely a big chunk of your problem. get it fixed before you cook the engine. vac leaks can cause lean running which can easily fry the pistons out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||jerpro||11-18-2000||02:06 PM||||I had the exact same problem. I changed the thermostat, the spark plug cables and the spark plugs themselves. <BR>My 2m4 is back to it.s rumling 500-1000 from a 2500 it was at before||||reg||6
Z||000016||jerpro||11-18-2000||02:13 PM||||I had the exact same problem. I changed the thermostat, the spark plug cables and the spark plugs themselves. <BR>My 2m4 is back to it.s rumling 500-1000 from a 2500 it was at before<P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||6
